paragraphs.paragraph_markdown:
The model extracts first-hop interaction sequences for source and destination nodes, padding them to a uniform sequence length $L_{	ext{src}}$ so that heterogeneous features—node identity, edge attributes, absolute temporal context, and co-occurrence signals—are embedded independently and stacked into a unified feature vector per interaction. A routing mechanism computes softmax weights over the sequence dimension; for the source node all weights are retained, while for the destination node only the top-$k$ weights are kept, bounded by $	ext{min}(k, L_{	ext{dst}})$. The final node embeddings are obtained as a weighted sum of the aligned feature vectors: $$
\begin{aligned} \mathbf{E}_{\text{src}} &= \sum_{i=1}^{L_{\text{src}}} \alpha_{\text{src},i} \cdot \mathbf{h}_{\text{src},i} \\ \mathbf{E}_{\text{dst}} &= \sum_{j=1}^{k} \alpha_{\text{dst},j} \cdot \mathbf{h}_{\text{dst},j} \end{aligned}
$$ where $	heta_{	ext{src},i}$ and $	heta_{	ext{dst},j}$ denote the routing weights for the $i$-th source interaction and the $j$-th selected destination interaction, $	ext{h}_{	ext{src},i}$ and $	ext{h}_{	ext{dst},j}$ are the aligned, stacked feature vectors, and $k$ is the number of top interactions selected for the destination node.

The timespan-aware forgetting mechanism decouples the discrete time step $Δt$ from input features and instead derives it as a function of the elapsed timespan $τ$ between the interaction and the prediction time, so that the state-space model forgets historical states proportionally to elapsed duration. The state transition matrix $A$ is initialized as $A_0$ with stable decay properties, ensuring that the hidden state decays smoothly over time rather than depending on input-driven gating. The input matrix $B$ and output matrix $C$ are redefined as $B_{	ext{spec}}$ and $C_{	ext{spec}}$ with spectral-norm constraints, which supports robust selective reviewing by bounding the per-interaction contribution to the state update. For temporal encoding, timespans from each interaction to the prediction time are mapped through a trainable cosine-based function, and node representations are obtained from raw features with zero vectors substituted when features are unavailable. The ordered first-hop neighbor events for a target node are extracted up to a prediction timestamp, providing the temporal sequence over which the SSM recurrence operates. The combined mechanism is formalized as $$
\Delta t = f_{\Delta t}(\tau), \quad A = A_0, \quad B = B_{\text{spec}}, \quad C = C_{\text{spec}}
$$, where $f_{Δt}$ denotes the mapping from timespan to step size and $A_0$ encodes the stable-decay initialization of the state transition matrix.

